                    The Open World
                    ==============

This version of NetHack has no staircases leading down from level to
level.  Instead, the world is one vast stretch of open country, and the
further you wander from where you started, the more dangerous it becomes.

The center of the world
    You begin standing on the high altar of your own god, at the center
    of a marble plaza.  The high altars of the other two gods stand to
    either side.  When you return here carrying the Amulet of Yendor and
    offer it (#offer) on your own god's high altar, you will ascend.
    The high altars accept no lesser sacrifice; offering a corpse there
    does nothing, and costs you nothing either.

Depth and the compass
    The land is divided into concentric rings around the center.  Your
    ring is your depth: "Dlvl" on the status line.  Monsters, treasure,
    traps, shops and everything else are chosen by depth just as they
    would be for a dungeon level that deep.  The status line also shows
    "Home:" followed by the direction and distance to the center, and a
    compass rose in the corner of the map points the way home.  Both can
    be turned off with the 'compass' option.

The lands
    Near the center lie gentle meadows.  Further out are forests and
    deep woods, hills, mountain ranges (solid rock, which can be dug
    through, walled by cliffs, with caves winding through the largest),
    lakes, swamps, frozen tundra, deserts with the occasional oasis, and
    the ruins of forgotten buildings.  Great roads lead outward from the
    plaza.  Towns hold shops, temples and houses, guarded by the town
    watch; in the deep country, towns may be abandoned and haunted.
    Treasure vaults are sealed inside the mountains; somewhere beyond
    depth 10, one of them may hold the portal to Fort Ludios.

Portals
    Special places are reached through magic portals, which stand in
    circles of standing stones evenly spaced around the ring at the
    depth where each belongs: the Gnomish Mines, the Oracle, Sokoban,
    the Big Room (if there is one), your Quest, Medusa's island, and in
    Gehennom the lairs of the demon lords, the towers of the Wizard and
    of Vlad, and the Gates of Moloch.  Larger rings have more portals.
    The name of the destination is engraved beside each portal.
    Leaving a branch (by its staircase or portal) returns you to the very
    portal you used to enter it.  #overview lists the portal rings you
    know about.

Other special places
    The world also holds places known to adventurers of other lands.
    Near the center: Goblin Town, the Mall, and the lairs of the Rat
    King and of Kroo the Kobold King.  Further out: Grund's Stronghold,
    Aphrodite's garden, the lairs of the Nightmare, the Beholder and
    Vecna (who guard great relics), the storerooms, the Wyrm Caves, the
    Lost Tomb, One-eyed Sam's black market, the Spider Caves, the guild
    of disgruntled adventurers, the Sunless Sea, the Temple of Moloch and
    the Giant Caverns, all before Medusa's island.  In Gehennom lie
    Frankenstein's laboratory and the lairs of Yeenoghu, Demogorgon,
    Geryon and Dispater.  Some of these exist only in some worlds.  The
    Gnome King holds court below Mines' End, and there is a dungeon
    beneath Fort Ludios.

    One-eyed Sam sells anything, at a price.  He won't serve customers
    in inhuman shapes, pets are not allowed in his market, and his
    assistants and guards deal with thieves.

Gehennom
    The outermost rings are Gehennom itself, walled off from the rest of
    the world by the Barrier, a ring of impassable, undiggable mountains.
    The only way through is the Valley of the Dead: take the portals to
    Medusa's island, pass her and the Castle beyond, and fall through the
    Castle's trap doors into the Valley.  The Valley's way down opens
    into Gehennom beyond the Barrier; gates there lead back into the
    Valley.  You cannot level teleport past the Barrier until you have
    entered Gehennom.  Deep in Gehennom, the invocation at the Gates of
    Moloch opens the way to the Sanctum.  Carrying the Amulet back up
    through Gehennom, a mysterious force may push you back out as you
    cross into a shallower ring.

Getting around
    The map scrolls so that you stay in the middle of the screen (see
    the 'centerview' option).  Number pad movement is on by default.
    Travel (_) works across the open country, and level teleportation
    moves you from one ring to another.  There is no "down": digging
    down makes a pit, and there are no trap doors or holes in the open
    world.  A cursed potion of gain level carries you one ring inward.
    Teleportation, and effects which normally cover "the whole level"
    (magic mapping, detection, clairvoyance), cover the surrounding
    stretch of country.

Monsters
    Monsters far from you rest where they are; only those within a
    screen or two of you are active.  So a dragon on the far side of the
    world will not come looking for you just because you share a level.

Roles, races and techniques
    Besides the usual thirteen roles you can play a Necromancer, Flame
    Mage, Ice Mage, Jedi, Pirate, Convict, Infidel or Cartographer, and
    besides the usual five races a giant, centaur, illithid, tortle,
    draugr, vampire, werewolf, doppelganger or ghost.  Every role and most
    races have techniques, special abilities learned as you gain
    experience: #technique (or M-x) lists them and uses one.

Materials
    Objects are made of materials, and many come in several: a silver
    long sword, a mithril chain mail, a wooden dagger.  The material
    changes weight, protection, damage, erosion and price.  There are no
    mithril coats; mithril is a material that body armor can be made of.
    Silver harms demons and the undead as always, cold iron harms elves,
    and copper harms molds and bringers of disease.

Skills
    #enhance shows each skill's training toward the next level as a
    percentage (100% is one level).
